{"question": "A 41-year-old G3P1 woman presents with a sudden onset throbbing headache, tinnitus, nausea, and left-sided weakness. Patient has no significant past medical history and takes no medications.  Her last two pregnancies ended with spontaneous abortions before the 10th week of gestation. No significant family history. Her vital signs include: blood pressure 130/90 mm Hg, pulse 58/min, respiratory rate 11/min, and temperature 36.8\u2103 (98.2\u2109). GCS is 14/15. Physical examination shows 3+ deep tendon reflexes and increased muscle tone in the left upper and lower extremities. Laboratory findings are significant for the following:\nPlatelet count 230,000/mm3\nFibrinogen 3.5 g/L\nActivated partial thromboplastin time  70 s\nThrombin time 34 s\n A non-contrast CT of the head is performed and shown in the picture. Which of the following would be the next best diagnostic step in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Mixing study", "B": "INR", "C": "D-dimer", "D": "Ristocetin-induced platelet aggregation test", "E": "Clot retraction study"}, "pred_answer": "C", "gold_answer": "A", "question_domains": ["Neurology", "Obstetrics and Gynecology", "Hematology", "Emergency Medicine", "Radiology"], "option_domains": ["Neurology", "Hematology"], "question_analyses": {"Neurology": "Based on the clinical presentation and the laboratory findings provided, the patient is exhibiting symptoms and signs that are concerning for a subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH). The sudden onset of a severe, throbbing headache (often described as the \"worst headache of my life\"), tinnitus, nausea, and left-sided weakness are classic symptoms of SAH. The physical examination findings, including increased muscle tone and deep tendon reflexes in the left upper and lower extremities, suggest a possible neurological deficit, which is consistent with SAH. The laboratory findings, particularly the platelet count of 230,000/mm\u00b3, are below the normal range (150,000-400,000/mm\u00b3), which could inc